# Line endings are this repository's decision, not the clone's.
#
# Git for Windows writes `core.autocrlf=true` at install time — the option worded
# "Checkout Windows-style, commit Unix-style line endings" — so a Windows clone gets CRLF
# for anything git reads as text unless something here says otherwise. Measured on this
# repository: `git -c core.autocrlf=true clone` produces 161 CRLF pairs in
# `extract-frames.sh`, and both ways of running it die.
#
#     ./extract-frames.sh        env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory      exit 127
#     bash extract-frames.sh     $'\r': command not found
#                                set: pipefail: invalid option name            exit 2
#
# `web-verify/SKILL.md` tells the user to run that file. It is the only shell script the
# pack ships — every other shell script was removed on Windows-portability grounds.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Nothing else is pinned, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. Every other
# tracked file is `.md`, `.py`, `.json`, `.txt`, `.yml` or `.toml`, and all of them read
# CRLF without complaint: Python's tokenizer, `json.load`, and every `read_text()` in this
# tree open in text mode, where universal newlines turn `\r\n` into `\n` before any caller
# sees it. A blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` would be safe today — measured: no tracked file
# is binary and none deliberately carries a carriage return — but it would be a rule
# written for a hazard nobody has, and this project has been paying for those.
#
# The one place line endings did matter off this list was the installer's manifest, which
# `write_manifest` now writes with `newline="\n"`. That belongs in the code: the file is
# generated on a user's machine and never passes through a checkout.
